## Dependency Injection Rules

```csharp
// Correct — attribute-based registration, Singleton lifetime
[DependencyInjection(typeof(IUserService))]
public class UserService : IUserService { ... }

// Wrong — manual registration in Startup for standard Service
services.AddScoped<IUserService, UserService>();
services.AddSingleton<IUserService, UserService>();
```

- Decorate **implementation** class with `[DependencyInjection(typeof(IXxx))]`
  — this replaces manual `services.Add*` in Startup, it is NOT about avoiding Singleton
- ECCore default lifetime is **Singleton** — do NOT override to Scoped or Transient
  for standard DomainService / Infrastructure types unless a written exception exists
- Interface project (`{SolutionName}.Interface`) holds declarations only — not registrations
- Use constructor injection; do NOT use `new` for injectable types

### Allowed manual registration in Startup (add comment explaining why)

- `AddHttpClient` / named HTTP clients
- `AddHostedService` / background workers
- Factory or singleton resources not mapping to a single interface
- Framework extensions requiring specific startup sequence
- Do NOT add `AddSwaggerGen` when Startup inherits `ECServiceStartup` (Swagger via `Swashbuckle.AspNetCore` 7.2.0 in `ECService`)
- `StartupBase` MUST implement `IStartup` — see ECGuide.md §2–§3

